>> It doesn't break game balance, because it only thing it breaks is the
>> amount of time required to clean out a shop.
>
>Which is still important; in the early game, most characters probably
>wouldn't have enough food to get Ks of $ out of random shopkeepers by
>the slower way you mentioned, unless they get lucky with their pets.

Not true. If you're unencumbered, you'll never starve in a nethack
game. You can take all the time you need.

The game allows for theft from shops. Exploiting it is a valid game
strategy. Whether or not you're stealing gold or items from a shop
it's turn efficient enough to get what you need.

>And in any case, whether or not I know what it is, it seems that credit
>cloning is more efficient, and could thus net the player more gold.

It may be more turn efficient. But if I have little or no gold, I simply
have the pet steal items instead and resell those. It works out to be
about the same amount of time/effort.

>As soon as food is no longer a problem, of course, this difference
>doesn't matter.

Food is rarely a problem. Any experienced nethacker knows how to manage
food well enough. The cost is generally not wearing jewelry, not casting
spells, and not being burdened. However early on, none of these are so
tough as not to spend a few hundred turns cleaning out a shop, which often
has food in them.

